    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, конечно, я знаю это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Максимилиан, вы понимаете французский язык?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно, я прекрасно понимаю французский.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Ну, что ты будешь делать теперь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я прочитаю этот рецепт и приготовлю блюдо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Это будет очень смешно.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let us break down this interaction.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Тут все по-французски.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here everything is in French.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'here' straight to 'in French'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Для нашего шефа это не проблема.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'For our chef this is not a problem.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'for' directly with 'problem'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Правда, Максимилиан?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Right, Maximilian?' Notice how 'honestly' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'person name'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Си.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Si.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'oui' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Си? Идиот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Si? Idiot.' Louis combines 'oui' with 'idiot' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Си — это по-итальянски.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Si is in Italian.' Louis links 'oui' directly with 'this' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "По-французски будет уи.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In French it will be oui.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'in French' with 'oui'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Да, конечно, я знаю это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, of course, I know this.' Notice how 'yes' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'this'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "vika",
      "t": "Максимилиан, вы понимаете французский язык?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Maximilian, do you understand the French language?' Elena uses the polite plural 'you' alongside 'language', keeping a formal or respectful register. Then Max responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно, я прекрасно понимаю французский.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course, I understand French perfectly.' Max links 'of course' directly with 'I' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Ну, что ты будешь делать теперь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, what will you do now?' Louis combines 'oh' with 'now' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я прочитаю этот рецепт и приготовлю блюдо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will read this recipe and prepare the dish.' Notice how 'I' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'dish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Это будет очень смешно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
